data/corpus.json.gz redistributes verbatim excerpts of KEP prose — specifically the
"Alternatives", "Drawbacks" and "Risks and Mitigations" sections — together with structured
metadata from kep.yaml and OWNERS files.

Modifications made to that material:
  - HTML comments stripped (the KEP template's own prompts)
  - "Alternatives" sections split into individual titled options
  - structural sub-headings ("Examples", "Conclusion", "Implementation sketch") excluded
  - OWNERS group aliases expanded to individual handles via OWNERS_ALIASES
  - bot accounts filtered
  - email addresses NOT ingested from the project's membership data (sigs.yaml), by choice

No personal data beyond the GitHub handles, display names and affiliations that the Kubernetes
project itself publishes as its governance record is included.

One qualification, because the sentence above would otherwise be slightly too clean. The verbatim
KEP excerpts are exactly that -- verbatim -- and one of them quotes a Linux kernel mailing-list
Message-ID that contains an address: `20180404115311.725-1-alban@kinvolk.io`, inside a
`Alternatives` discussion of rootless container builds. It was published by the Kubernetes project
in its own repository, and it is a citation to a public mailing-list thread rather than contact
details harvested from a membership file.

Two promises in this file pull against each other there: excerpts are reproduced verbatim, and
email addresses are not ingested. Redacting it would break the first; pretending it is not there
would break the second. So it is named here. It is the only one -- `eval_gold.py` counts
email-shaped strings in both the committed corpus and the live graph on every run, and fails if
that count moves.
